---
import Site from "../layouts/Site.astro";
import BoardGallery from "../components/BoardGallery.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const parts = [
  {
    file: "neopixel.png",
    name: "An addressable RGB pixel",
    description:
      "A single pixel shown close up. For this build, buy a prewired strip or ring containing 8–16 pixels; the data travels from one to the next.",
  },
  {
    file: "breadboard.png",
    name: "Breadboard",
    description:
      "Its connected holes hold a temporary circuit. Use the board with soldered headers; the bare edge holes alone will not make a reliable connection.",
  },
  {
    file: "jumper-wires.png",
    name: "Jumper wires",
    description:
      "Plug-in leads join the circuit. Choose male or female ends to suit your board headers, breadboard and modules.",
  },
  {
    file: "resistor-330r.png",
    name: "Data resistor",
    description:
      "A 330 Ω resistor is shown. Use 330–470 Ω in the data line near the first LED; it protects the signal connection, not the strip’s power supply.",
  },
  {
    file: "capacitor.png",
    name: "Electrolytic capacitor",
    description:
      "Shape example only: buy 500–1000 µF, rated at least 6.3 V. The stripe marks the negative side. Check the value and polarity on your own part.",
  },
];
---

<Site
  title="Build your first lights"
  description="Build a small LightVM lighting project: choose a board, gather LEDs, cables and power, connect the parts and try your first pattern."
>
  <section class="wrap page-intro narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow">A BOARD, A FEW LIGHTS, YOUR IDEAS</p>
    <h1>Build your first lights.</h1>
    <p class="intro">
      See the parts, follow the connections and make a small system you can
      explain.
    </p>
    <p>
      This starter project uses a Raspberry Pi Pico 2 W and a short strip or
      ring of individually addressable LEDs: each light can have its own colour.
      LightVM is the firmware — the software on the board — that runs your
      patterns. You are assembling ready-made parts, not designing a circuit
      board from scratch. Along the way, follow the power, the computation and
      the data signal: each is a different part of making the lights work.
    </p>
    <nav class="guide-links" aria-label="In this build guide">
      <a href="#before-you-buy">Before you buy</a>
      <a href="#meet-the-boards">Board pictures</a>
      <a href="#parts">Parts list</a>
      <a href="#connections">Connections</a>
      <a href="#first-pattern">First pattern</a>
    </nav>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap narrow" id="before-you-buy">
    <div class="testing-note">
      <p class="eyebrow">EARLY TESTING</p>
      <h2>Check the software before buying the hardware.</h2>
      <p>
        This is an experimental build, not a finished kit. Check the{" "}
        <a href={`${base}releases/`}>release catalogue</a> for an installable
        LightVM build for your exact board. If none is available, hardware setup
        currently means compiling firmware with the developer tools. The{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/KajLights/lightvm/blob/main/docs/pico2-w.md">
          Pico 2 W developer guide
        </a>{" "}
        covers that route; it needs a local development environment.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can start today in the{" "}
        <a href="https://kajlights.github.io/lightvm/">
          browser lighting playground
        </a>{" "}
        with no board. Try an example and its on-screen preview before deciding
        to buy parts. Features and instructions are still being tested, and
        changes may require wiping and setting up a test board again.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section" id="meet-the-boards">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">RECOGNISE WHAT IS IN YOUR HAND</p>
        <h2>Meet the boards.</h2>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="section-lede">
      The Pico 2 W is the example below. The other photos help you recognise
      supported alternatives; use their own firmware and pin guide. A similar
      shape does not mean the same wiring. Check the model printed on the board.
    </p>
    <BoardGallery />
    <div class="subjects board-anatomy">
      <article>
        <h3>USB socket</h3>
        <p>
          The silver socket connects the board to your computer for power and
          data. Pico boards use Micro-USB; these XIAO boards use USB-C.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Microcontroller</h3>
        <p>
          The processor and memory run the program. On a Pico the main chip is
          visible; on these XIAO boards it is under the metal shield.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Pads and headers</h3>
        <p>
          The rows along the edges connect to other parts. GP16 names a
          programmable signal; GND is ground. Fitted header pins make these
          connections easier to plug in.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="parts-in-pictures">
    <p class="eyebrow">A FEW OF THE SMALLER BITS</p>
    <h2 id="parts-in-pictures">What do the components look like?</h2>
    <div class="component-gallery">
      {parts.map((part) => (
        <figure class="component-card">
          <img
            src={`${base}assets/hardware/${part.file}`}
            alt={part.name}
            width="600"
            height="500"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>
            <h3>{part.name}</h3>
            <p>{part.description}</p>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="image-credit">
      Component images: Raspberry Pi Foundation, CC BY 4.0.{" "}
      <a href={`${base}image-credits/`}>Sources and licences</a>. These pictures
      help identify types of parts; the list below specifies the values to buy.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section narrow prose" id="parts">
    <h2>The bits you’ll need</h2>
    <ul class="parts-list">
      <li>
        <strong>
          One Raspberry Pi Pico 2 W, preferably with fitted headers.
        </strong>{" "}
        Headers are the rows of metal pins that let you plug the board into a
        breadboard. Use{" "}
        <a href="https://www.raspberrypi.com/products/raspberry-pi-pico-2/">
          Raspberry Pi’s retailer finder
        </a>{" "}
        and select the exact model. Already own a board? Check the{" "}
        <a href={`${base}hardware/`}>other supported models</a>; their firmware
        and pin names may differ.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A computer and a USB data cable.</strong> The Pico 2 W uses
        Micro-USB; choose a cable that carries data, not a charge-only cable.
        Desktop Chrome or Edge is the simplest starting point for the browser’s
        USB device tools. Copying a Pico firmware file to its USB drive does not
        need those browser tools.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A small 5 V WS2812B-compatible RGB strip or ring.</strong>{" "}
        Around 8–16 pixels is a manageable first project. Check the voltage, LED
        type and pixel count in the listing. Choose RGB, not RGBW or an ordinary
        non-addressable strip, for this example. Fitted leads make connecting it
        easier.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A breadboard, jumper wires and suitable LED connectors.</strong>{" "}
        A breadboard holds temporary connections without soldering. If your
        board has no headers or the LEDs have only bare pads, you will also need
        headers or leads, a soldering iron and solder — or help fitting them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A 3.3 V-to-5 V data level shifter.</strong> Use a ready-made
        74AHCT125-based module with supply decoupling, suitable for WS2812 data,
        and follow its pin and enable instructions. This raises the board’s data
        signal to the level expected by 5 V LEDs.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>
          A 330–470 Ω data resistor and a 500–1000 µF capacitor rated at least
          6.3 V.
        </strong>{" "}
        The resistor goes near the first LED’s data input; the capacitor goes
        across the LED power input. Check whether your LED or interface module
        already includes these parts.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>
          A regulated 5 V DC supply for the LEDs, with a suitable connector.
        </strong>{" "}
        Size it for the strip manufacturer’s maximum current for your pixel
        count, including full white. Power the Pico from the computer’s USB for
        this setup. The LEDs get their own power connection; the board’s GPIO
        and 3V3 pins do not power the strip.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The level shifter, resistor and capacitor follow{" "}
      <a href="https://learn.adafruit.com/adafruit-neopixel-uberguide/best-practices">
        Adafruit’s NeoPixel wiring guidance
      </a>
      . Its{" "}
      <a href="https://learn.adafruit.com/adafruit-neopixel-uberguide/powering-neopixels">
        power guide
      </a>{" "}
      explains how to choose a supply. Start with a ready-made, enclosed
      low-voltage adapter. A microphone, extra sensors, enclosure and Wi-Fi
      setup can all wait until after your first pattern.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section prose" id="connections">
    <h2>Connect the first few lights</h2>
    <p>
      Unplug USB and the LED supply while making connections. Use the{" "}
      <a href="https://www.raspberrypi.com/documentation/microcontrollers/pico-series.html">
        official Pico pin diagram
      </a>{" "}
      and your level-shifter module’s instructions. This example uses{" "}
      <strong>GP16</strong>, the signal name printed in the pin diagram, not
      physical pin number 16.
    </p>
    <figure class="wiring-figure">
      <a
        href={`${base}assets/hardware/pico2w-wiring.svg`}
        aria-label="Open the wiring diagram at full size"
      >
        <img
          src={`${base}assets/hardware/pico2w-wiring.svg`}
          width="1280"
          height="680"
          alt="Computer to Pico by USB. Pico GP16 to level shifter, then resistor, then LED DIN. A separate 5 V supply powers the shifter and LEDs; all grounds join. Capacitor positive goes to LED 5 V and negative to ground."
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </a>
      <figcaption>
        Read the labels as well as the colours: purple is USB, teal is LED data,
        red is +5 V and dark grey is ground. This shows connections, not
        breadboard holes or physical pin positions.{" "}
        <a href={`${base}assets/hardware/pico2w-wiring.svg`}>
          Open the full-size diagram ↗
        </a>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="concept-note">
      <h3>Three things to notice</h3>
      <p>
        The strip’s power comes from the 5 V supply. Its instructions come from
        GP16 through the level shifter. Ground gives the board, shifter and LEDs
        a shared electrical reference. The capacitor sits across the power
        input; the resistor sits in the data path.
      </p>
    </div>
    <table class="wiring-table">
      <caption>Pico 2 W with a separate 5 V LED supply</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">From</th>
          <th scope="col">Connect to</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Pico GP16</th>
          <td>The level shifter’s data input.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Level shifter’s output</th>
          <td>
            The data resistor, then the LEDs’ DIN (data in). Follow the arrows
            along the strip away from the board.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Module output enable (OE), if exposed</th>
          <td>
            The 74AHCT125 enable is active-low: connect it to GND. Some modules
            already do this. Follow the module’s own pin diagram.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">External 5 V supply +</th>
          <td>The LEDs’ 5 V input and the level shifter’s 5 V supply input.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">External supply − / ground</th>
          <td>
            The LEDs’ ground, level shifter’s ground and a Pico GND pin, all
            connected together.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Capacitor</th>
          <td>
            Across the LED power input: + to 5 V and − to ground. Observe the
            polarity marked on it.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Pico Micro-USB</th>
          <td>Your computer, for the board’s power and data.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Keep the data lead short. In this two-supply setup, join the grounds but
      leave external 5 V disconnected from the Pico’s power and GPIO pins. Check
      connections and capacitor polarity before powering up. Switch on the LED
      supply before connecting the Pico’s USB; disconnect USB before switching
      off the LED supply.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section narrow prose" id="first-pattern">
    <h2>From assembled parts to your first pattern</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Install firmware for the exact board.</strong> Follow{" "}
        <a href={`${base}install/`}>Set up a device</a> if an approved release
        is available, or the developer guide linked above. For Pico 2 W, hold
        BOOTSEL while connecting USB and copy its LightVM UF2 file to the{" "}
        <strong>RP2350</strong> drive. A Pico W firmware file will not work on a
        Pico 2 W. Firmware installation may erase existing experiments.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>
          Connect in the lighting editor and configure the output.
        </strong>{" "}
        Open the{" "}
        <a href="https://kajlights.github.io/lightvm/">LightVM editor</a>,
        select the board’s USB connection, and open Configuration. Add a WS2812B
        output with pin <strong>GP16</strong>, your actual pixel count and the
        strip’s colour order (commonly GRB). Set a low brightness limit for the
        first test and save the configuration.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Try a simple colour, then a pattern.</strong> Preview the Color
        example on the board. If red appears green, check colour order. If
        nothing lights up, unplug power and check the data direction, shared
        ground and chosen pin. Once it works, try Rainbow or a beginner lesson
        and change one thing at a time.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Keep what you make.</strong> Save your editable source to your
        computer. Deploy an animation and save a device preset when you want the
        board to keep it; choose its startup behaviour in Configuration if you
        want it to run again after power is restored.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Need help with the editor? The{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/KajLights/lightvm/blob/main/docs/web-editor.md">
        workspace guide
      </a>{" "}
      explains its controls. If you find a gap in this build guide,{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/KajLights/KajLights.github.io/issues">
        share the model, parts and step you reached
      </a>
      . That is useful feedback while the project is finding its feet.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section narrow prose">
    <h2>Now investigate what you made</h2>
    <p>
      Predict what will change when you alter the timing or colour. Trace the
      instruction from your program through GP16 to the first pixel. Explain why
      the power wire still matters when the program is correct.
    </p>
    <p>
      When you are ready, extend the same investigation to messages over USB,
      Wi-Fi or MQTT. Each connection has rules and limits to understand.
    </p>
    <a class="text-link" href={`${base}learn/`}>
      Continue with electronics, computing and communications →
    </a>
  </section>
</Site>
